Running yoyo --provider openai with no model now uses gpt-6-sol instead of gpt-5. The guessed-limits warning is gone for openai: a user who only picks the provider now gets the model's real limits and a real /cost, not a 4096-token cap on every reply. I committed it as b0c7e7ab with "Part of #1037" in the title. Build, every test suite, clippy with -D warnings and the fmt check all pass.

Changes:
• `src/providers.rs`: the openai default is now gpt-6-sol, with a comment explaining the choice. I didn't pick gpt-6.1-sol (what Codex ships) because it has no preset or price yet, so it would bring the warning back. A new test checks the same three things the anthropic default was checked for on Day 230: the model has a preset, it has a price, and its default output size is within the model's maximum.
• `src/model_limits_note.rs`: a new test runs the warning's own check over every provider's built-in default (all of KNOWN_PROVIDERS except ollama, where the user picks the model). The 7 that still warn are openrouter, xai, groq, mistral, github, zai and cerebras. That matches the assessment's table, so the list can only get shorter by the number fixed.
• Docs: three pages listed the openai default, and all still said gpt-4o: installation.md, models.md and guides/fork.md. All now say gpt-6-sol.
• One correction to the plan: the plan said the "gpt-5" test at cli.rs:4958 was an explicit --model argument and should be left alone. It wasn't. test_parse_fallback_derives_model checks the fallback model, which comes from the same default, so it failed in the full run and I updated it. The commit body says so.

Checks:
• Sabotage test: I put the default back to gpt-5 with a marker, ran the tests and restored the file in one command. Both new tests failed by name, then passed after the restore, and no marker was left behind.
• Live run: in an empty temp dir with a dummy key, the built binary printed model: gpt-6-sol and no "guessed limits" line. The call itself then failed with a 401, as expected.
